    summary: "Perou, Sørlie, Eisen, van de Rijn and colleagues characterised gene expression in 65 surgical specimens from 42 individuals using complementary DNA microarrays representing 8,102 human genes. Each tumour had a distinctive molecular portrait; 20 tumours sampled before and after 16 weeks of doxorubicin, and two paired with a lymph node metastasis, showed that two samples from the same person were almost always more alike than either was to any other tumour. Sets of co-expressed genes tracked specific features of the tumours, and the tumours could be classified into subtypes distinguished by pervasive differences in expression.",
